It radically, radically accelerates your time to market.

73
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.040
And while we're talking design, we have to talk about item potency.

74
00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.740
Don't let the big word scare you.

75
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.680
Item potency simply means that no matter how many times you run an operation,

76
00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.760
the result is the exact same as if you ran it just once.

77
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.160
So why does the business care?

78
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.460
Well, imagine a customer taps buy, right?

79
00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.600
But then their Wi-Fi drops and their app automatically retries the request.

80
00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.640
Without item potency, congratulations, that customer just got charged twice.

81
00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:26.100
Total nightmare. But by implementing an item potency key, the server recognizes the duplicate

82
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.640
request and guarantees your customer is only charged once. Giants like Stripe and Square rely

83
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.400
heavily on this. It's absolutely essential. Section four, versioning and stability. Once

84
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.060
your API is live in the wild, you have to establish critical rules so you don't accidentally break

85
00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:46.020
your partner's integrations overnight. This is basically the ultimate survival checklist for

86
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:52.680
NEPM managing an API. Understanding breaking versus non-breaking changes. Adding a new optional

87
00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.920
field or throwing in a completely new endpoint, totally fine. That's non-breaking. But the second

88
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.000
you remove an existing field or change a data type from a string to a number or suddenly make

89
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.140
a previously optional field mandatory, you've just instituted a breaking change. And you are

90
00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:13.880
going to crash your client's software. Not good. Because of that massive risk, we follow the golden

91
00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:20.240
rule of API management. Once published, never break. Period. If you absolutely must make a

92
00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.320
breaking change to fundamentally improve the system, you have to release an entirely new API

93
00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.240
version, like moving from version one to version two. And here's the kicker. You have to keep

94
00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.980
supporting that old version for a minimum of 12 to 18 months. You have to give your partners time

95
00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:39.280
to migrate gracefully. Section five, security and integration. This is where product managers

96
00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.380
set up the guardrails to protect the business while still smartly connecting it to the outside

97
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:49.460
world. Security naturally starts with authentication, basically verifying exactly who is calling your

98
00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:54.700
system. In different business scenarios, demand completely different methods. A simple API key,

99
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.300
that might be totally fine for a quick internal service. But if you're building a public app where

100
00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:04.390
third parties are accessing user data, you absolutely need OAuth 2.0. If you're handling

101
00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:09.890
heavily secure financial B2B integrations, you're going to need mutual TLS, where both servers

102
00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:14.950
verify each other's certificates. As a PM, your job is to match the security rigor directly to the

103
00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:21.730
business use case. Next up is rate limiting. If you don't limit how often clients can call your API,

104
00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:27.110
a single poorly coded app can send thousands of requests a second and completely crash your

105
00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:32.590
servers for everyone else. You can set up tiered limits. Maybe your free users get 100 requests an

106
00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:38.070
hour while enterprise users get unlimited access. Twitter is a great real world example here. They

107
00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:42.530
actively protect their platform by capping developers at 300 requests per 15-minute window

108
00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:46.730
for their really intensive search endpoints. You have to design these limits intentionally.

109
00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:53.390
Now let's talk about how systems communicate efficiently using webhooks. A webhook is essentially

110
00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:58.230
a reverse API. Instead of your app constantly knocking on the server's door every five seconds

111
00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:02.790
asking, hey, is there an update? Is there an update? Which, by the way, wastes an immense

112
00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:07.750
amount of resources. A webhook allows the server to proactively push that real-time event to your

113
00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:12.470
client the exact millisecond it happens. This is how GitHub alerts your system of a code update

114
00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:17.390
or how Stripe instantly tells your server a payment went through. It is so much more efficient.

115
